issue.body:
The same issue as https://github.com/eslint/eslint/issues/4164 but I was asked to make a new issue. This is still a problem for this code:

```
            foo(
              () => {
                bar();
              }, () => {
                baz();
              }
            );
```

It complains with 

```
  357:17  error  Expected indentation of 14 space characters but found 16  indent
  362:15  error  Expected indentation of 12 space characters but found 14  indent
```

body:
I am not sure what the right thing here is but if we try to fix the issue above then we have to start marking below example as an error.

``` js
require('http').request({hostname: 'localhost',
                         port: 80}, function(res) {
  res.end();
});
```

this is already in our unit test as a valid case: https://github.com/eslint/eslint/blob/master/tests/lib/rules/indent.js#L101-L104

@eslint/eslint-team thoughts?

Bringing in another use case to add to the original issue: #4184 (since its related)

``` js
            foo(
              () => {
                bar();
              }, () => {
                baz();
              }
            );
```

@wli: Your expected output looks not quite right to me, I see no compelling reason to wrap and indent the arguments there. How about something like this:

``` js
showUpdateContactModal(contactType, contactId, function() {
  selectContact(contactType, contactId); // 339
  refreshEmergencyForm(); // 340
}); // 341
```

@silverwind that's a good workaround for this particular case, but it looks like there are competing styles if you have a lot of arguments and you need to line wrap. Basically, this ticket and https://github.com/eslint/eslint/issues/4206 are at odds as to where to put the inner function indent level.

Option A - line length is clearly too long. this should always throw an error.

```
happens.to.be.some.very.very.long.widgetSerializer(one, two, three, four, five six, seven, eight, nine, ten, eleven, function() {
  // function code is here
});
```

Option B - to fit within line length, we indent the arguments at the position of the original argument. It's starting to look rather odd.

```
happens.to.be.some.very.very.long.widgetSerializer(one, two, three,
                                                   four, five six, 
                                                   seven, eight,
                                                   nine, ten,
                                                   eleven, function() {
  // function code here?
                                                     // or here?
});
```

Option C - arguments are no longer at the same indent level, which makes it more difficult to parse

```
happens.to.be.some.very.very.long.widgetSerializer(one, two, three, four,
  five six, seven, eight, nine, ten, eleven, function() {
    // function code here?
  // or here?
  });
```

Option D - you can see all the arguments in one line

```
happens.to.be.some.very.very.long.widgetSerializer(
  one, two, three, four, five six, seven, eight, nine, ten, eleven, function() {
    // function code here?
  // or here?
});
```
